es that can form chows/pungs easily. W4+W5 can form chow with two different tiles (W3 or W6). W1 alone only pairs with one other W1 (hard to get) or forms chow with W2-W3 (unlikely). T7 already is a pair, can become pung.  \n- So keep T7 (pair/pung), keep W4 and W5 (chow possibility), discard W1.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nDiscarding W1 preserves flexibility:  \n- W4-W5 can become a chow with W3 or W6 (7 total available tiles).  \n- T7 can become pung (3 tiles left) or remain as pair.  \n- W1 is isolated, only useful as pair (needs one W1) or chow with W2-W3 (W2 only 1 left).  \n- W1 discard is relatively safe (only 3 left, not many in others' hands likely).  \n- Keeping T7 may allow Two Dragon Pungs? No, F2 is already pung but dragons are J1,J2,J3, so not relevant.  \n- Need to aim for at least 8 points: Possible patterns after completing hand: All Types (6 pts) + Dragon Pung? No + Seat Wind Pung (F2 is not wind; Seat Wind is West/F3, so no) + maybe Self-Drawn (1 pt) + maybe All Chows (2 pts) \u2192 but I have a pung (F2), so All Chows not possible. Mixed Straight maybe? Not yet. Might need Last Tile or other patterns.  \n\nBut first, need to complete hand. Discarding W1 is optimal for tile efficiency.  \n\n**Answer:** Play W1"}
