iles must make 1 meld (since I already have 3 melds made and T7 pair). But 1 meld needs 3 tiles, so 4 tiles means I have 3 + 1 extra waiting tile for that meld.  \n\nLet\u2019s break it:  \nCurrent: 3 melds exposed, hand has 5 tiles: W4, W5, W7, T7, T7.  \n\nDiscard one tile:  \n- If discard W4: hand = W5, W7, T7, T7 \u2192 wait: need W6 to form W5,W6,W7 chow. Then T7 pair. That\u2019s 1 possible winning tile (W6).  \n- If discard W5: hand = W4, W7, T7, T7 \u2192 wait: W5,W6,W7 not possible; maybe W4,W5,W6 needs W5 and W6 (2 tiles), not good. Or pung T7 (needs 1 more T7) or pung W7 (needs 2 W7s). This is bad.  \n- If discard W7: hand = W4, W5, T7, T7 \u2192 wait: W4,W5,W6 chow (needs W6). That\u2019s same as first scenario.  \n- If discard T7: hand = W4, W5, W7, T7 \u2192 wait: pair is T7 & one of W4/W5/W7? No, I\u2019d need a pair from W tiles and a meld from 3 others, but currently have 4 different tiles \u2014 W4,W5,W7,T7. That\u2019s 4 different tiles. To win I need to make a meld (3 tiles) and a pair (2 tiles). The only possible meld is W4,W5,W6 (needs W6 and discard T7), but then pair would be W7? No, W7 only one left. So discard T7 leaves me with no pair unless W7 becomes pair later. I\u2019d need another W7 to pair W7, and need W6 to chow W4,W5,W6 \u2014 this is two different draws (W6 and W7), bad shape.  \n\nThus the best waits are **discard W4 or W7 \u2192 wait for W6**, but both also let me wait for T7 pung? Actually if discard W4: hand W5,W7,T7,T7. Can win with:  \n   (A) W6 \u2192 meld W5,W6,W7, pair T7 \u2192 standard hand.  \n   (B) T7 \u2192 meld T7,T7,T7, pair W5? No, pair would be W5 and W7 leftover \u2014 that\u2019s not a pair. So not valid. So only W6.  \n\nIf discard W7: hand W4,W5,T7,T7 \u2192 only W6 works, for chow W4,W5,W6.  \n\nSo in both cases, waiting for 1 tile type (W6).  \n\nLet\u2019s check W6 availability:  \nUnshown tiles list shows W6: 4 left. Good availability.  \n\nAlso need to consider scoring patterns:  \n- Already have F2 pung \u2192 that\u2019s Dragon? No, F2 is South Wind, not Dragon. Prevailing wind and seat wind = West (F3). F2 is not wind bonus.  \n- Chow B6-B8 and T2-T4: All simples (no terminals/honors except F2).  \n- If win with W6 chow: all tiles will be simples except the F2 pung. That gives:  \n   1. All simples (2 points)  \n   2. No honors (1 point) \u2014 but F2 is an honor? Yes, wind is honor. So \u201cNo honors\u201d not satisfied.  \n   3. Mixed chow hand likely.  \n\nMinimal fan count is F2 pung = Pung of terminals or honors (1 point), All simples (2 points) = 3 points. Need at least 8 points to win. This current hand is far from 8 points unless I get other patterns like concealed hand, self-drawn, etc.  \n\nGiven that, maybe I should change plan \u2014 but changing plan now is impossible with exposed melds.  \n\nThus with exposed melds, maybe try for:  \n- Last Tile (4 points)  \n- Self-drawn (1 point)  \n- Edge Wait? No. Closed Wait? Possible if W6 is middle of chow W5,W6,W7, but here W6 is middle if I have W5,W6,W7, yes. Closed Wait (2 points) if winning tile W6 is middle.  \n\nStill far from 8.  \n\nBut maybe I overlooked: if discard W5 (hand W4,W7,T7,T7):  \nWait for W5 (to chow W4,W5,W6), need W5 and W6, bad.  \nWait for T7 (to pung T7), then leftover W4,W7 not a pair, so need W4 or W7 as pair, so need draw W4 or W7 as well. Very bad.  \n\nSo best is indeed waiting for 1 tile type W6, by discarding W4 or W7.  \n\nBetween W4 and W7:  \nUnshown: W4: 2 left, W7: 3 left. Possibly safer to discard the one less in hand originally? I have 1 of each. Discard W7: keeps W4,W5 for chow, requires W6. Discard W4: same.  \n\nGame history: previous player had W2,W3,W4 chow? No, next player has meld W2,W3,W4. Previous player hasn\u2019t shown much W4/W7.  \n\nBut safer discard? In terms of defensiveness, W7 is slightly safer because W4 is closer to W5-W6 chow people might keep for sequences.  \n\nI\u2019ll choose to keep W4,W5 (because W4,W5 needs W6 to complete chow; W5,W7 also needs W6 but W5,W7 is a worse shape because gap). Actually:  \nIf I keep W5,W7: gap needs W6 only, same as W4,W5 needing W6. So equally good. But discarding W4 leaves W5,W7: both not touching W4 (someone might need W4 for chow with W2,W3,W4).  \n\nBut next player already has W2,W3,W4 meld, so W4 is dead to him.  \n\nLet\u2019s check discards: My history shows I played W3 earlier, W1 earlier. Other players: next played W5, W7. Previous played W2\u00d72, W4 once.  \n\nGiven next player\u2019s W2,W3,W4 meld, he probably won\u2019t need W4/W5 now.  \n\n**Conclusion**:  \nI will discard W7 because W7 is middle of higher numbers (7), and less likely to be needed for chow given many W7 already out. Also I have T7 pair already, reducing need for W7 as pair for others.  \n\nEvaluation: The most promising move is to discard W7 to wait for W6 to complete chow W4,W5,W6, giving a 1-tile wait with good tile availability (4 left).  \n\nReason: Keeping W4 and W5 allows a single W6 to complete the meld. Discarding W7 is safer than discarding W4 in terms of not giving middle tiles to others\u2019 sequences, and it equalizes wait efficiency.  \n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
